---
/**
 * Accessibility statement.
 *
 * URL deliberately matches the original site: /accessibility-statement.
 *
 * The original page is an UNEDITED Wix template — it still contains the
 * template's own authoring instructions. Rather than carry that across, this
 * page states what is actually true of this build, all of it measured:
 * contrast ratios, keyboard access, heading structure, reduced-motion
 * support. Keep it honest — if a claim stops being true, change the claim.
 */
import BaseLayout from '../layouts/BaseLayout.astro';
import PageHeader from '../components/PageHeader.astro';
import Section from '../components/Section.astro';
import Prose from '../components/Prose.astro';
import Reveal from '../components/Reveal.astro';
import { company } from '../data/site';

const lastUpdated = 'August 2026';
---

<BaseLayout
  title="Accessibility Statement"
  description="Urjapod Energy is committed to making this website usable by everyone, including people with disabilities. What we have done, known limitations, and how to report a problem."
  breadcrumbs={[
    { label: 'Accessibility Statement', href: '/accessibility-statement/' },
  ]}
>
  <PageHeader
    eyebrow="Accessibility"
    title="Accessibility statement"
    lead="We want everyone to be able to use this site — including people browsing with a keyboard, a screen reader, magnification, or with motion sensitivity."
  />

  <Section width="narrow">
    <Reveal>
      <Prose>
        <h2>Our commitment</h2>
        <p>
          Urjapod Energy is working to make this website accessible to people
          with disabilities. An accessible site lets visitors with disabilities
          browse with the same ease and enjoyment as everyone else. We treat
          accessibility as part of building the site, not something bolted on
          afterwards.
        </p>

        <h2>What we have done</h2>
        <p>
          This site was built to the Web Content Accessibility Guidelines
          (WCAG) 2.1 Level AA. Specifically:
        </p>
        <ul>
          <li>
            <strong>Colour contrast.</strong> Every text and background pair on
            the site was measured, not estimated, and meets or exceeds the
            4.5:1 ratio required for normal text. Our brand amber is used only
            as a graphic or behind dark text, never as light text, because it
            cannot pass contrast on a white background.
          </li>
          <li>
            <strong>Keyboard access.</strong> Every link, button and form field
            can be reached and operated with a keyboard alone. Focus outlines
            are never removed, and a “Skip to content” link is the first thing
            you reach when you press Tab.
          </li>
          <li>
            <strong>Structure.</strong> Each page has a single main heading and
            a heading order that does not skip levels, so screen reader users
            can navigate by headings. Landmarks — header, main, footer,
            navigation — are marked up so they can be jumped between.
          </li>
          <li>
            <strong>Names for controls.</strong> Every link and button has a
            meaningful accessible name. Links that open in a new tab say so.
            Icons that carry meaning are labelled; decorative graphics are
            hidden from assistive technology so they are not read out as noise.
          </li>
          <li>
            <strong>Reduced motion.</strong> If your operating system is set to
            reduce motion, all animation and smooth scrolling is switched off
            automatically.
          </li>
          <li>
            <strong>Zoom and small screens.</strong> Text can be enlarged
            without breaking the layout, pinch-zoom is not disabled, and no
            page scrolls sideways at common screen widths.
          </li>
          <li>
            <strong>Forms.</strong> Fields have visible labels rather than
            placeholder-only hints, errors appear in text next to the field
            they belong to, and focus moves to the first problem when a
            submission fails.
          </li>
          <li>
            <strong>Works without JavaScript.</strong> All content and contact
            details remain readable if scripts fail to load. Only the contact
            form needs JavaScript, and our email address and phone number are
            shown as plain text beside it.
          </li>
        </ul>

        <h2>Known limitations</h2>
        <p>We would rather name the gaps than claim there are none:</p>
        <ul>
          <li>
            The chemistry comparison table on the Sodium Ion page is wider than
            a phone screen and scrolls sideways inside its own frame. It is
            reachable by keyboard and labelled as a scrollable region, but a
            wide table is still harder to read on a small screen.
          </li>
          <li>
            This site has not yet been audited by an independent accessibility
            specialist, nor formally tested with every screen reader and
            browser combination.
          </li>
        </ul>

        <h2>Tell us if something does not work</h2>
        <p>
          If any part of this site is difficult or impossible to use, we want
          to hear about it — it is the fastest way for us to fix it. Email
          {' '}<a href={`mailto:${company.email}`}>{company.email}</a> or call
          {' '}<a href={`tel:${company.phoneHref}`}>{company.phone}</a>, and
          tell us the page and what went wrong. We will reply and let you know
          how we intend to put it right.
        </p>
        <p>
          If you need information from this site in a different format, contact
          us and we will provide it.
        </p>

        <h2>This statement</h2>
        <p>
          This statement applies to <strong>www.urjapod.com</strong> and was
          last reviewed in {lastUpdated}. We review it whenever the site
          changes.
        </p>
      </Prose>
    </Reveal>
  </Section>
</BaseLayout>
